<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>

  <main class="history-page">

    <!-- =========================================================
         HERO
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="hero">

      <div class="hero-inner">

        <div class="hero-copy">

          <p class="eyebrow">
            Homeopathy in History
          </p>

          <h1>
            William James
          </h1>

          <p class="hero-subtitle">
            Physician. Psychologist. Philosopher.
          </p>

          <p class="hero-kicker">
            What happens when we follow the evidence across forty years?
          </p>

          <p class="hero-intro">
  William James (1842–1910), the Harvard physician, psychologist,
  and philosopher whose work helped shape modern psychology and
  American pragmatism, left behind an unusually revealing documentary
  record of his encounters with homeopathy—one that stretches from
  his medical training into the final years of his life.
</p>

        </div>


        <figure class="hero-portrait">

  <img
    src="/images/history/william-james-portrait.jpg"
    alt="Portrait of William James, 1903"
    loading="eager"
  />

  <figcaption>
    William James, 1903. Photograph by Notman Studios.
    Houghton Library, Harvard University,
    MS Am 1092 (1185). Public domain.
  </figcaption>

</figure>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         INTRODUCTION
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="article-section reading-column opening">

      <p class="lead">
        William James’s relationship with homeopathy is more interesting
        than either a simple endorsement or a simple rejection.
      </p>

      <p>
        James earned his medical degree from Harvard in 1869, but his
        intellectual life soon moved far beyond conventional medical practice.
        He became one of the central figures in the development of American
        psychology and philosophy, publishing
        <em> The Principles of Psychology</em>
        in 1890 and
        <em> The Varieties of Religious Experience</em>
        in 1902.
      </p>

      <p>
        His thinking was marked by a persistent interest in experience:
        what people actually observed, what happened when an idea was tested,
        and whether accepted theories adequately accounted for the facts.
      </p>

      <p>
        Homeopathy entered that story remarkably early—and it continued
        to appear throughout much of his life.
      </p>

      <p>
        Rather than beginning with later claims about what James supposedly
        believed, I followed the documentary record backward:
        <strong>
          James’s medical notebooks, family correspondence, published
          letters, public writings, and surviving manuscripts.
        </strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        Read chronologically, they reveal a relationship with homeopathy
        extending from his years as a young physician into the final years
        of his life.
      </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         MEDICAL SCHOOL NOTEBOOK
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="feature-band">

      <div class="reading-column">

        <p class="section-label">
          c. 1868–1869
        </p>

        <h2>
          A Medical Notebook Headed<br />
          “Similia Similibus Curantur”
        </h2>

        <p>
          The earliest evidence we have found comes from James’s own
          medical-era notebooks.
        </p>

        <p>
          Among the surviving William James papers at Harvard is
          <strong> Notebook 26</strong>, a collection of notes begun in the
          1860s and continued over a period of years.
        </p>

        <p>
          Scholarship examining James’s reading notes identifies an entry
          filed under the Latin phrase
          <strong>
            <em> “Similia similibus curantur”</em>
          </strong>—
          “like cures like,” the principle at the center of homeopathy.
        </p>

        <p>
          The notes appear to draw in part upon James’s medical reading,
          including Frederick William Headland’s
          <em> On the Action of Medicines in the System</em>.
        </p>

        <p>
          This should not be read as proof that the young James had already
          embraced homeopathy.
          What it does show is that the principle was among the therapeutic
          questions he considered seriously enough to enter into his
          medical notes.
        </p>


