      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         WILKINSON 1869
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="article-section reading-column">

      <p class="section-label">
        September 1869
      </p>

      <h2>
        James Becomes the Patient
      </h2>

      <p>
        The documentary trail becomes more personal almost immediately
        after James completed his medical degree.
      </p>

      <p>
        James John Garth Wilkinson—a physician, family friend, and prominent
        nineteenth-century homeopath—became involved in William’s care.
      </p>

      <p>
        The surviving evidence comes not from a later biographer but from
        William’s own family correspondence.
      </p>

      <p>
        On September 21, 1869, William’s mother,
        <strong> Mary Walsh James</strong>, wrote to his brother Henry.
        Scholarship citing the manuscript records her discussion of
        Wilkinson’s
        <strong> “diagnosis &amp; prescription” </strong>
        and identifies the medicines as
        <strong> high dilutions of Rhus and Nux vomica</strong>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Henry subsequently wrote to William that he was
        <strong> “extremely interested” </strong>
        in what their mother had reported about Wilkinson’s diagnosis
        and prescription.
      </p>


      <aside class="archive-placeholder">

        <span class="archive-status">
          Primary Manuscript Requested
        </span>

        <h3>
          Mary Walsh James to Henry James Jr.
        </h3>

        <p>
          September 21, 1869.
          Complete surviving item: 2 sheets / 8 pages.
        </p>

        <p class="archive-citation">
          Houghton Library, Harvard University<br />
          MS Am 1093.1 · Box 1 · Item 37
        </p>

        <p class="archive-note">
          Once the Houghton scan arrives, the wording concerning Wilkinson,
          Rhus, and Nux vomica will be transcribed directly from the original
          letter and displayed here.
        </p>

      </aside>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         1872 HYDRASTIS
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="feature-band soft">

      <div class="reading-column">

        <p class="section-label">
          1872
        </p>

        <h2>
          “A Homeopathic Remedy”
        </h2>

        <p>
          A few years later, James’s own correspondence provides another
          intriguing piece of the record.
        </p>

        <p>
          In a letter to his brother Henry, James reported that he had found
          a homeopathic preparation of
          <strong> Hydrastis </strong>
          beneficial for constipation.
        </p>

        <blockquote class="pull-quote">

          <p>
            “I have found a homeopathic remedy, hydrastis … to be of
            decided efficacy…”
          </p>

        </blockquote>

        <p>
          This is significant because James was no longer merely reading
          about a therapeutic principle or hearing about a prescription
          chosen for him.
        </p>

        <p>
          He was describing a remedy he himself identified as homeopathic
          and reporting the result he believed he had experienced.
        </p>


        <aside class="evidence-note">

          <h3>
            Source Verification in Progress
          </h3>

          <p>
            This passage is preserved in William James scholarship and
            correspondence references, but the exact original manuscript
            has not yet been independently examined for this project.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before publication, the archival location and complete
            transcription will be verified.
          </p>

        </aside>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         1898 MEDICAL REGISTRATION ADDRESS
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="article-section reading-column">

      <p class="section-label">
        February 1898
      </p>

      <h2>
        Homeopathy Enters James’s Public Argument
      </h2>

      <p>
        Nearly three decades after Wilkinson treated him, James confronted
        homeopathy from a very different position.
      </p>

      <p>
        Massachusetts was debating legislation that would regulate who
        could legally practice medicine.
        James opposed the proposed system because he believed professional
        licensing could suppress forms of investigation and experience
        before their value had been fairly tested.
      </p>

      <p>
        In his
        <em> Address on the Medical Registration Bill</em>,
        James specifically raised homeopathy.
      </p>


      <blockquote class="quote-card">

        <p>
          “An enormous mass of experience, both of homeopathic doctors and
          their patients, is invoked in favor of the efficacy of these
          remedies and doses.”
        </p>

      </blockquote>


      <p>
        The importance of the statement lies in what James was—and was
        not—arguing.
      </p>

      <p>
        He was not declaring that every claim made for homeopathy had
        therefore been proved.
      </p>

      <p>
        He was challenging the assumption that such a body of reported
        experience could simply be dismissed without investigation.
      </p>

      <p>
        For James, the question was methodological:
        <strong>
          should medicine close inquiry by definition, or allow experience
          to test the theory?
        </strong>
      </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         PUTNAM LETTER
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="primary-source">

      <div class="reading-column">

        <p class="section-label">
          March 2, 1898
        </p>

        <h2>
          “Why Seek to Stop” the Experiences?
        </h2>

        <p>
          Only days after his appearance at the Massachusetts State House,
          James wrote privately to neurologist
          <strong> James Jackson Putnam</strong>.
        </p>

        <p>
          The private letter helps explain the principle behind his public
          argument.
        </p>


        <blockquote class="major-quote">

          <p>
            “Why seek to stop the really extremely important experiences
            which these peculiar creatures are rolling up?”
          </p>

        </blockquote>


        <p>
          The wording is characteristically Jamesian.
          He could be skeptical, humorous, even irreverent toward people
          whose claims did not fit established categories.
        </p>

        <p>
          But ridicule was not, for him, a substitute for investigation.
        </p>

        <p>
          The accumulated experiences themselves remained evidence that
          deserved examination.
        </p>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         1903 RANKIN LETTER
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="article-section source-feature">

      <div class="side-layout two-column-left">

        <figure class="side-image side-left letter-scan">

          <img
            src="/images/history/james-rankin-letter.jpg"
            alt="William James's handwritten February 27 1903 letter to Henry William Rankin discussing homeopathic remedies"
            loading="lazy"
          />

          <figcaption>
            William James to Henry William Rankin,
            February 27, 1903.
            Houghton Library, Harvard University,
            MS Am 2355 (30).
          </figcaption>

        </figure>


        <div class="reading-column">

          <p class="section-label">
            February 27, 1903
          </p>

          <h2>
            The Manuscript Changes the Story
          </h2>

          <p>
            Five years after the medical-registration debate,
            James addressed homeopathy again—this time in a private
            handwritten letter.
          </p>

          <p>
            On February 27, 1903, James wrote to
            <strong> Henry William Rankin</strong>.
          </p>

          <p>
            The original manuscript survives in the Houghton Library at
            Harvard University.
          </p>

          <blockquote class="pull-quote">

            <p>
              “I always believe that homeopathy should get a fair trial in
              obstinate chronic cases.”
            </p>

          </blockquote>

          <blockquote class="pull-quote second">

            <p>
              “I know that homeopathic remedies are not inert, as orthodox
              medicine insists that they necessarily must be.”
            </p>

          </blockquote>

          <p>
            The distinction matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            James was not merely defending someone else’s right to experiment.
            He now made a direct statement about the remedies themselves.
          </p>

          <p>
            He still did not become an advocate for every doctrine associated
            with homeopathy.
            But neither did he regard the medicines as pharmacologically
            meaningless.
          </p>


          <aside class="evidence-note">

            <h3>
              Reading the Original Handwriting
            </h3>

            <p>
              Published transcriptions of this letter have sometimes rendered
              James’s first sentence as
              <strong> “I always believed…”</strong>
            </p>

            <p>
              Examination of the Houghton manuscript for this project appears
              instead to support
              <strong> “I always believe…”</strong>
            </p>

            <p>
              The manuscript image is reproduced so readers can examine
              James’s handwriting for themselves.
            </p>

          </aside>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         1909 TAYLOR
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="feature-band">

      <div class="reading-column">

        <p class="section-label">
          1909
        </p>

        <h2>
          James Returns to Homeopathic Care
        </h2>

        <p>
          The documentary trail does not end with the Rankin letter.
      </p>

        <p>
          By 1909, James’s health had become increasingly difficult.
          During this period he came under the care of
          <strong> Dr. James Ralph Taylor Jr.</strong>,
          a Boston physician educated at Boston University during the era
          of its homeopathic medical school.
        </p>

        <p>
          James’s letters to his brother Henry describe Taylor in initially
          skeptical terms, but they also document a sustained course of
          treatment.
        </p>

        <p>
          The regimen was not exclusively homeopathic.
          James described a mixture of approaches that included homeopathic
          medicines along with several physical and electrical treatments.
        </p>

        <p>
          The surviving correspondence is therefore particularly valuable:
          it allows Taylor’s homeopathic treatment to be documented without
          pretending that it was the only therapy James received.
        </p>


        <blockquote class="quote-card">

          <p>
            James referred specifically to Taylor’s
            “homoeopathic pellets.”
          </p>

        </blockquote>


        <p>
          What seems to have impressed James increasingly, however, was not
          simply a medicine.
          It was Taylor’s interpretation of the way James lived.
        </p>

        <p>
          James wrote admiringly of Taylor’s
          <strong> “extraordinarily shrewd perceptions” </strong>
          concerning what he called
          <strong> “the dynamics of the human machine.” </strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          Taylor believed James habitually lived at too intense a pitch:
          racing mentally, anticipating events, bracing himself against
          circumstances, and carrying unnecessary tension into both thought
          and movement.
        </p>

        <p>
          James eventually summarized Taylor’s purpose in striking language:
          the real task, he wrote, was
          <strong>
            “to re-educate me as to my general way of holding myself in the
            current of life.”
          </strong>
        </p>


